.TH "zelta\-failover" "8" "" "" "System Manager\(cqs Manual"
.SH NAME
\f[B]zelta failover\f[R] \- promote a backup target through a guarded
ZFS failover workflow
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]zelta failover\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] \f[I]source\f[R]
\f[I]target\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]zelta failover\f[R] composes the normal active\-passive promotion
workflow for a dataset tree.
It locks the active source, performs a final backup, syncs local ZFS
properties, and unlocks the promoted target.
.PP
The command is intended for planned promotion and controlled failback
workflows where the standby dataset tree is already maintained by
\f[B]zelta backup\f[R].
It does not make split\-brain safe by itself; operators must still
ensure only one side is active and writable.
.PP
When reciprocal \f[B]zelta policy\f[R] jobs maintain both directions,
this is the Zelta Twin pattern: an asynchronous cluster pair where
either dataset tree can become active after a guarded failover.
.PP
The individual commands are also useful outside the complete failover
workflow.
The complete command reference follows below.
.SS Composable Administrative Commands
The failover workflow is composed from several useful administrative
commands.
Use \f[B]zelta failover\f[R] when you want the complete guarded
promotion, or use the individual commands when you need to inspect,
repeat, or combine one operation with another administrative workflow.
.SS \f[B]zelta lock\f[R]
\f[B]zelta lock\f[R] applies ordered readonly, canmount, unmount, and
remount operations to a dataset tree.
It is useful after a partial failover, before maintenance, or whenever a
dataset tree needs to be made safely inactive.
.PP
\f[B]zelta lock\f[R] \f[I]endpoint\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-f\f[R], \f[B]\-\-force\f[R]
Force unmounts during lock.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-no\-unmount\f[R]
Set readonly and canmount state but leave mounted filesystems mounted.
.IP
.EX
zelta lock primary.example.com:tank/service
.EE
.SS \f[B]zelta propsync\f[R]
\f[B]zelta propsync\f[R] replays local ZFS properties from one dataset
tree to another while preserving target\-only local overrides.
It can prepare a promoted target to take over service, or make the
properties of an unrelated dataset tree match another tree.
.PP
\f[B]zelta propsync\f[R] \f[I]source\f[R] \f[I]target\f[R]
.IP
.EX
zelta propsync primary.example.com:tank/service standby.example.com:tank/service
.EE
.SS \f[B]zelta unlock\f[R]
\f[B]zelta unlock\f[R] reverses the lock state so a promoted dataset
tree can become active.
It is also useful when bringing a cloned or otherwise prepared dataset
tree online in a controlled way.
.PP
\f[B]zelta unlock\f[R] \f[I]endpoint\f[R]
.IP
.EX
zelta unlock standby.example.com:tank/service
.EE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[I]source\f[R]
Active dataset tree to lock and back up before promotion.
.TP
\f[I]target\f[R]
Standby dataset tree to receive the final backup and become writable.
.TP
\f[B]\-n\f[R], \f[B]\-\-dryrun\f[R], \f[B]\-\-dry\-run\f[R]
Display underlying commands without executing them.
.TP
\f[B]\-v\f[R], \f[B]\-\-verbose\f[R]
Increase verbosity.
Specify once for operational detail, twice for debug output.
.TP
\f[B]\-q\f[R], \f[B]\-\-quiet\f[R]
Decrease log output.
.TP
\f[B]\-f\f[R], \f[B]\-\-force\f[R]
Force source unmounts during the lock phase.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-no\-unmount\f[R]
Lock the source read\-only but leave mounted filesystems mounted.
.PP
Backup transport, send, receive, resume, bookmark, and snapshot naming
options accepted by \f[B]zelta backup\f[R] may also be passed to
\f[B]zelta failover\f[R].
The failover command uses them for the final backup step; see
\f[B]zelta\-backup(8)\f[R] for details.
.SH EXAMPLES
Promote a standby service tree:
.IP
.EX
zelta failover primary.example.com:tank/service standby.example.com:tank/service
.EE
.PP
Promote using source\-initiated remote transport:
.IP
.EX
zelta failover \-\-push primary.example.com:tank/service standby.example.com:tank/service
.EE
.PP
Perform the same workflow explicitly:
.IP
.EX
zelta lock primary.example.com:tank/service
zelta backup primary.example.com:tank/service standby.example.com:tank/service
zelta propsync primary.example.com:tank/service standby.example.com:tank/service
zelta unlock standby.example.com:tank/service
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success, 1 if non\-critical mount or unmount operations
fail after the state transition continues, 2 if the final backup fails
after source lock, and 255 if Zelta cannot safely set or clear dataset
readonly state.
.PP
Unmount failures on the locked source and mount failures on the unlocked
target are reported but do not stop failover.
Readonly and final backup failures stop immediately.
.SH NOTES
Always verify state with \f[B]zelta match\f[R] before and after
promotion.
Do not run both sides read\-write at the same time.
